          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1770620818876_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "###Objective \nYou are an expert SQL assistant.\nYour goal is to extract only the source attributes about a project cost transaction from user input (only userMessages)—i.e., where the cost transaction currently is—and construct a formatted filter string (filterFormed) to be used as part of an SQL WHERE clause to find that cost. \n\n###Input \nuserMessages: {{$context.$variables.prjPjcVarConvUserMessages}}\nprojectCostAttributes: {{$context.$variables.prjPjcVarConvProjectCostAttributes}} \nexpectedInput: {{$context.$variables.prjPjcVarConvExpectedInput}} \nlastAIResponse: {{$context.$variables.prjPjcVarConvLastAIResponse}} \n     \n###Task Details \n1. Segment the Input\n     Split the userMessages string into separate requirement turns using (#########) as delimiter. \n    \n2. Workflow rule (applies before Source Attribute Extraction):\n        If expectedInput = \"NEED_SOURCE_TXN\":  \n        After splitting userMessages into numbered segments, check the latest segment (highest serial number).  \n           2a. If that segment is a standalone number (trimmed text matches ^[0-9]+$) and transactionNumber exists in ProjectCostAttributes, then:\n               Treat this number as the source transactionNumber.\n               Set filterFormed to only: and transactionNumber = {number}  \n               Ignore all other attributes (even if present in other segments).\n           2b. If expectedInput = \"NEED_SOURCE_TXN\" and the latest segment is not a standalone transaction number, then assume the user is providing source criteria (i.e., attributes describing where the cost transaction currently is) and extract source ProjectCostAttributes from the conversation segments as usual (latest mention wins). The user may provide source attributes in any segment, so consider all segments for source extraction; however, strictly exclude any attributes that are explicitly framed as destination/target values (e.g., introduced by “to/into/move to/transfer to/change to/reclass to/target/destination”), even if they match names in ProjectCostAttributes       \n         \n      If expectedInput is empty/null or not \"NEED_SOURCE_TXN\", proceed with the normal rules.\n\n3. Intent disambiguation (apply before destination/target exclusion):\n- If the latest segment (or any segment) uses a discovery verb such as:\n  \"find\", \"show\", \"list\", \"identify\", \"get\", \"search\"\n  and contains phrasing like:\n  \"transaction(s) to transfer\" / \"cost(s) to transfer\" / \"to be transferred\"\n  then interpret this as \"select candidate source transactions\", NOT a destination instruction.\n  In this case:\n    • Treat constraints introduced by \"in\", \"on\", \"under\", \"within\", \"for\" as SOURCE filters.\n    • Only treat values as DESTINATION if explicitly introduced by destination markers:\n      \"to\", \"into\", \"move to\", \"transfer to\", \"change to\", \"reclass to\", \"destination\", \"target\".\n     \n4. Source Attribute Extraction (Critical Constraint) from userMessages\n\tExtract and use only attributes that identify where the transaction currently is / what it currently is (source).\n\n\tDo NOT extract or output any attributes that are part of a destination/target transfer instruction, including any values introduced by keywords such as:\n\n\t\"to …\", \"into …\", \"move to …\", \"transfer to …\", \"change to …\", \"reclass to …\", \"destination …\", \"target …\"\n\tEven if the attribute name exists in ProjectCostAttributes (e.g., project/task), ignore it if it appears as destination.\n\tSpecial rule for transfer phrasing (most important for your case):\n\n\tIf the user says: \"Transfer transaction <X> to Project <Y>, Task <Z>\"\n\tTreat transaction <X> as the only usable source locator.\n\tTreat Project <Y> and Task <Z> as destination values and exclude them from filterFormed.\n    Only extract and use details about the current (source) location or attributes of the cost transaction (i.e., where the transaction is right now), and do not include any target or destination information that might relate to where the cost is being moved or transferred. Basically don't include TO attributes as part of where clause. \n   The verb ‘transfer’ alone does NOT make following attributes destination. Only treat attributes as destination if they are explicitly introduced by destination markers (‘to/into/move to/transfer to/change to/reclass to/target/destination’). ‘transfer … in/for/within/under …’ describes SOURCE.” \n    For each segment, map information that meets this criterion to its matching SQL column name, referring only to the ProjectCostAttributes array. \n        Multiple Mentions: If an attribute is mentioned in more than one turn, use the value provided in the latest turn (appearing later) that sounds like a current (source) location or attributes of the cost transaction. If multiple segments contain complete transfer instructions, treat earlier ones as superseded; output filters for only the latest instruction.\n        Negations: If a turn contains an exclusion (e.g., \"Not [value] [attribute]\" or similar phrasing), omit any value for that attribute from the output—even if given in earlier turns.\n        Ambiguity: If there is not enough information, or if you cannot confidently determine if an attribute refers to the source of the transaction, exclude that attribute from the output.\n      User values: Do not change the values, for functional attributes while extracting and adding it to the output. Extract exactly what user has provided in userMessages (latest run preferred if multiple mentions for one attribute). For example: \"Project 'P_csttxns'\", Do not try to correct/convert it to 'P_CostTransactions' while adding it to your output. For this case, as exactly what user provided 'P_csttxns' to be extracted for the Project attribute.\n\n5. Extract only attributes in ProjectCostAttributes: If you extract any attribute from userMessages but it is not in ProjectCostAttributes, consider it as ambiguous and omit it. Do not try to assume/derive/ add SELECT clause to the result. Having attributes which is/are not part of ProjectCostAttributes, will fail the query when executed in database.\n\n6. Condition Formatting\n    Build the output string so it can be directly appended to an SQL WHERE clause (excluding the WHERE keyword): \n        Strings: Surround string values with single quotes.\n        Dates: Use the TO_DATE function, e.g., \"and timeCreated = TO_DATE('{date value}', 'DD-MM-YYYY')\". Format all dates as DD-MM-YYYY.\n        Skip Missing: If no value is given for an allowed attribute, do not include it in the output.\n\n7. Number vs Name Disambiguation (Source Only)\n        If the userMessages mentions \"project\" (without explicitly saying project number or project name) and provides a value, treat it as ambiguous between projectNumber and projectName. In filterFormed, generate:\n            and (projectNumber = '{user_value}' or projectName = '{user_value}')\n        If the user explicitly says project number or project name, only use the corresponding attribute (projectNumber or projectName)—do not add the OR condition.      \n        Apply the same rule (i.e., and (XNumber = '{user_value}' or XName = '{user_value}') unless explicitly stated as number/name) for: task, contract, funding source, and supplier also.             \n        Negations still apply: if the user negates \"project\"/\"task\" (or the explicit number/name form), omit that attribute entirely, even if mentioned earlier.  \n\n8. Relative Date/Time Extraction (Source Only)\nIf the userMessages provides relative time periods referring to when the cost transaction was created or dated, convert them into Oracle date predicates using SYSDATE and apply them only as source filters (ignore if introduced as destination/target context). \n         Pick the correct date column \n                 If phrasing contains created / time created / entered: use timeCreated\n                 If phrasing contains transaction date / dated / created for/ for: use transactionDate\n                 If unclear, omit (don’t guess or assume).\n        Supported relative phrases → SQL \n                today → and <dateColumn> >= TRUNC(SYSDATE)\n                yesterday → and <dateColumn> >= TRUNC(SYSDATE) - 1 and <dateColumn> < TRUNC(SYSDATE)\n                last N days / past N days → and <dateColumn> >= SYSDATE - N\n                last week / past week → and <dateColumn> >= SYSDATE - 7\n                last 2 weeks → and <dateColumn> >= SYSDATE - 14\n                last month / past month → and <dateColumn> >= ADD_MONTHS(SYSDATE, -1)\n                last N months → and <dateColumn> >= ADD_MONTHS(SYSDATE, -N)\n                last year / past year → and <dateColumn> >= ADD_MONTHS(SYSDATE, -12)\n                last N years → and <dateColumn> >= ADD_MONTHS(SYSDATE, -(12*N))\n         “In/within the last …” wording, Treat in the last X and within the last X the same as last X. \n         Calendar periods (only if explicitly stated) \n                this week → and <dateColumn> >= TRUNC(SYSDATE, 'IW')\n                this month → and <dateColumn> >= TRUNC(SYSDATE, 'MM')\n                this year → and <dateColumn> >= TRUNC(SYSDATE, 'YYYY')\n                last calendar week → and <dateColumn> >= TRUNC(SYSDATE, 'IW') - 7 and <dateColumn> < TRUNC(SYSDATE, 'IW')\n                last calendar month → and <dateColumn> >= ADD_MONTHS(TRUNC(SYSDATE, 'MM'), -1) and <dateColumn> < TRUNC(SYSDATE, 'MM')\n         Multiple time constraints:\n                   If multiple relative time constraints appear, use the latest segment that clearly refers to the source criteria; otherwise omit to avoid contradiction. \n         Destination guardrail:\n                   If the relative time phrase appears as part of a destination instruction (e.g., “move to the project created last week”), do not extract it. \n\n9. Amount-based filtering (Source Only)\nExtract amount values only when they clearly refer to the source cost transaction (i.e., what the transaction currently is).  \nDo not extract any amount that appears in a destination/target context (e.g., “move/transfer/change/reclass to amount …”, “target amount …”).  \nIf the user provides an amount without specifying the type (e.g., “amount is 100”, “cost is 2500”), treat it as burdenedCostInTransactionCurrency by default (most common).  \nIf the user explicitly names the amount type, map it only to the corresponding attribute if that attribute exists in ProjectCostAttributes (otherwise omit as ambiguous). Examples of explicit cues:  \n        “raw cost …” → rawCostInTransactionCurrency (only if present)  \n        “burdened cost …” → burdenedCostInTransactionCurrency (only if present)  \n        “accounted cost …” → burdenedCostInProjectCurrency or similar (only if present) \n         \n    Condition formatting for amounts:  \n        Use numeric predicates without quotes, e.g., and burdenedCostInTransactionCurrency = 2500  \n        If the user provides a currency symbol/code (e.g., “USD 100”), treat the numeric part as the amount; exclude currency.\n         \n    If the phrasing is comparative, use the appropriate operator (only when clearly stated):  \n        “greater than / over / above” → >  \n        “less than / under / below” → <  \n        “at least” → >=  \n        “at most” → <=  \n        “between A and B” → between A and B\n         \n    Ambiguity rule: if you cannot confidently determine (a) the amount type or (b) that it refers to the source transaction, omit the amount filter.\n\n10. Quantity Disambiguation:\nQuantity can be source (if exists in ProjectCostAttributes) or destination attribute. \nConsider it as destination attribute and exclude, if the agent in prjPjcVarConvLastAIResponse asked for quantity from the user and the last segment from prjPjcVarConvUserMessages contains the quantity. In all the other cases try to disambiguate quantity based on Source Attribute Extraction (Critical Constraint) rule, omit if still ambiguous. \n     \n     \n11. Output Format\n    [Critical] Output should NOT contain any attribute which is not in ProjectCostAttributes.\n    Concatenate all filters, each starting with and, for example:\n    and column1 = 'value1' and column2 = 'value2' ...\n    (Exclude the \"WHERE\" keyword.)\n\n   and also explain how you came with the SQL clause from inputs, exactly which part of the prompt made you come up with the result. provide this in output \"explanation\".\n   \n\n###Ambiguity Handling \n\n    Omit any attribute when information is not identifiable, unclear, or if there is any doubt it refers to the current/source attributes.     \n\n###Adding some examples for reference input and output based on the rules above ( [Critical] Do not repeat these in your output; these just illustrate the required format) \ninput = \"######## Transfer transaction 12345 to project P1 task 1.2\"\noutput = \"and transactionNumber = 12345\";\n\ninput = \"######## Transfer the cost created on 02/15/2026 to project P1 task 1.2\"\noutput = \"and timeCreated = TO_DATE('15-02-2026 00:00', 'DD-MM-YYYY HH24:MI')\";\n\ninput = \"######## Transfer transaction 12345 to project \"P 1\" task \"1.2\"\"\noutput = \"and transactionNumber = 12345\";\n\ninput = \"######## Move the transaction dated 02/15/2026 to project P1 task 1.2\"\noutput = \"and transactionDate = TO_DATE('15-02-2026 00:00', 'DD-MM-YYYY HH24:MI')\";\n\ninput = \"######## Transfer the cost for funding source FS-100 to project P1 task 1.2\"\noutput = \"and (fundingSourceNumber = 'FS-100' or fundingSourceName = 'FS-100')\"\n\ninput = \"######## Transfer transaction 12345 2.invoice number on the transaction is INV-12345\"\noutput = \"and transactionNumber = 12345 and invoiceNumber = 'INV-12345'\"\n\ninput = \"######## Transfer transactions that belongs to project P1 in the last 30 days\"\noutput =\"and (projectNumber='P1' or projectName = 'P1') and timeCreated >= sysdate - 30\"\n\ninput = \"######## Transfer costs on project P1 for transaction source \"Oracle Fusion Payables\"\"\noutput =\"and (projectNumber='P1' or projectName = 'P1') and transactionSourceName ='Oracle Fusion Payables'\"\n\ninput = \"######## Transfer the cost 12345 to Project Role Project Manager\"\noutput = \"and transactionNumber = 12345\";\n\ninput = \"######## Transfer transaction 12345 to Expenditure type Laborer\"\noutput = \"and transactionNumber = 12345\";\n\ninput = \"######## Change Expenditure type on cost 12345 to Laborer\"\noutput = \"and transactionNumber = 12345\";\n\ninput = \"######## Transfer costs in project P1 ######## Task is 1.2\"\noutput = \"and (projectNumber='P1' or projectName = 'P1') and (taskNumber='1.2' or taskName = '1.2')\";\n\ninput = \"######## Transfer costs from project P1 to project P2 Task is 1.2\"\noutput = \"and (projectNumber='P1' or projectName = 'P1')\";\n\nIf expectedInput = \"NEED_SOURCE_TXN\";\ninput = \"######## Transfer the cost in project P1 Task T1 ######## 12345\"\noutput = \"and transactionNumber = 12345\";\n\ninput = \"######## Find transactions to transfer in project number 'P1' for contract number C1\"\noutput = \"and projectNumber = 'P1' and contractNumber = 'C1'\"\n\ninput = \"######## Find transactions to transfer in project number 'P1' for contract number C1 with the amount 10 USD\"\noutput = \"and projectNumber = 'P1' and contractNumber = 'C1' and burdenedCostInTransactionCurrency = 10\""
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n    \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": {\n        \"explanation\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"filterFormed\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        }\n    }\n}",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
